          "dom_locator": "/html/body/div[7]/div/div/div/div/div[2]/div/div[3]/div[1]/div[1]/div[2]/div[3]",
          "source_sha256": "988dcbe103a9af3a736656be5d535be631ba0abf486ba88eec9fd7b230a69419"
        }
      ],
      "verified_table_blocks": [
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 8,
          "literal_text": "My 76 disjoined species belong to 34 families,—and I cannot see that they incline to belong to small families. diag 15 are Gramineæ which form 113 of our Flora."
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 9,
          "literal_text": "18 ’ Cyperaceæ ’ 110 ’"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 10,
          "literal_text": "The 1 Leguminosa & 1 Composita are as you would like; but that is because these orders are remarkable for their species being of narrow range."
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 11,
          "literal_text": "3 are Rosaceæ"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 12,
          "literal_text": "2 ’ Scrophulariaceæ (the 1 orchid is to be erased)"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 13,
          "literal_text": "3 ’ Ranunculaceæ. &c &c"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 14,
          "literal_text": "6 ’ Umbelliferæramme"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 15,
          "literal_text": "As to our trees, what proportion have flowers more or less separated. Number the orders on p. 400— 1. Magnoliaceæ, and so on. And append diag p.= polygamous more or less. m = monœcious d = diœcious."
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 16,
          "literal_text": "separated flowers 1. Magnoliaceæ 0 2 — 0 3 — 0 4 — 0 5 — 1 p 6 — 8 p. 7 — 2 d 8 — 0 9 — 1 m 10 — 1 p 11 — 2 p. d. 12 0 13 0 14 1-p 15 1 p 16 0 17 — 7 p. d. 18 — 2 p 19 — 8 p. d. 20 — 1 m 21 — 9 m 22 — 21 m. 23 — 5 m. 24 7 d 25 Coniferæ— 18 m. d.ramme"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 17,
          "literal_text": "Out of 132 trees, those with separated flowers more or less—are 95.—and for the greater part very decidedly separated."
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 18,
          "literal_text": "I must think it by chance—that your introduced plants are in so near the proportion by families that the indigenous species are. diag Indigenous Introduced Our Compositæ 18 — nearly 110."
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 19,
          "literal_text": "’ Cyperaceæ 110 — [ 160 ]"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 20,
          "literal_text": "’ Gramineæ 113 — 18"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 21,
          "literal_text": "’ Leguminosæ 124 — 118"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 22,
          "literal_text": "’ Rosaceæ 129 — 152. &"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 23,
          "literal_text": "’ Orchidaceæ – 0"
        },
        {
          "retained_body_paragraph": 24,
          "literal_text": "’ Ranunculaceæ 143 — 143 but ’ Labiatæ 143 — 111!ramme"
        }
      ]
    }
